---
import Shell from "../../layouts/Shell.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../site";

/**
 * The three ways to run the PyTorch snippets, compared honestly.
 *
 * This page carries an affiliate link, so it is written to be useful to
 * somebody who ignores it. The comparison says plainly that running locally is
 * free and faster, and that nothing in these tutorials is big enough for a GPU
 * to help — both of which argue against the paid option, and both of which are
 * measured rather than guessed. A reader who cannot tell whether a
 * recommendation is bought will not trust the parts that were not, and this
 * site has nothing else to sell.
 *
 * The disclosure renders off SITE.railway.paid, so it appears exactly when the
 * link actually earns and can never be forgotten.
 */
const description =
  "Three ways to run the PyTorch snippets on this site: in your browser on NumPy, on your own machine, or on a small deployed server if you cannot install Python locally. What each costs, and which is fastest.";

const rows = [
  {
    what: "In this page",
    how: "Already running",
    cost: "Free",
    speed: "2.4 ms/step",
    catch: "NumPy, not PyTorch. This is what every tutorial part uses.",
  },
  {
    what: "Your own machine",
    how: "pip install torch",
    cost: "Free",
    speed: "0.5 ms/step",
    catch: "Needs Python and about 2 GB of disk. Safari cannot connect to it.",
  },
  {
    what: "A deployed runner",
    how: "One Dockerfile",
    cost: "~$5/month",
    // Not measured, and not guessed at in the table either. It depends on the
    // container size, and inventing a figure here would be the one thing this
    // page cannot afford while it also carries a paid link.
    speed: null,
    catch: "Works from a Chromebook, an iPad, or a locked-down work laptop.",
  },
];
---

<Shell
  title={`Running the PyTorch snippets — ${SITE.name}`}
  description={description}
  canonical="/pytorch/run-it/"
  reference
>
  <div class="pb-24">
    <nav class="mono-note flex flex-wrap gap-x-2 pt-10 text-graphite" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-plot">Tutorials</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/pytorch/" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-plot">PyTorch</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="mt-8 border-b pb-8 hairline">
      <p class="plate-no">Reference</p>
      <h1
        class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl leading-[1.12] font-extrabold tracking-[-0.02em] sm:text-4xl"
      >
        Running the PyTorch snippets
      </h1>
      <p class="lede measure mt-5">
        The tutorials need none of this. They run in the browser on NumPy and
        always will. This is only for the reference pages, where the point is
        the library itself.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose mt-12">
      <h2>Which one do you want?</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Where</th>
            <th>Setup</th>
            <th>Cost</th>
            <th>Speed</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>{r.what}</strong>
                  <span class="mt-1 block text-graphite">{r.catch}</span>
                </td>
                <td>{r.how}</td>
                <td>{r.cost}</td>
                <td>
                  {r.speed ? <code>{r.speed}</code> : <span class="text-graphite">not measured</span>}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="mt-6">
        Those two figures are measured, on one training step of the transformer
        the later parts build — same code, same size, both places. Your own
        machine wins, and it is free. If you can install Python, install Python.
      </p>

      <p>
        The deployed row says <em>not measured</em> because it is not. It
        depends on the container you rent, and I am not going to put a number
        next to the option I am paid to recommend without having run it. Expect
        somewhere between the other two: native PyTorch, on a CPU that is
        probably slower than your laptop's.
      </p>

      <h2>Do you need a GPU?</h2>

      <p>
        No, and paying for one here would be worse than not. The model in these
        tutorials has a few thousand parameters. Moving numbers to a graphics
        card and back costs more time than arithmetic at that size saves, so a
        GPU is slower for this and bills by the hour for the privilege.
      </p>

      <p>
        That changes the moment you train on real text at real width, which is
        past where this tutorial stops. Until then, a $5 CPU container is the
        whole of what is worth renting.
      </p>

      <h2>Running it on your own machine</h2>

      <p>Two commands, and nothing leaves your laptop.</p>

      <pre><code>{`curl -O https://learn.welldun.ai/runner/welldun_runner.py
pip install torch
python welldun_runner.py`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        It prints a URL with a token. Paste that into any reference page and the
        Run buttons come alive. The runner binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code>, so
        nothing outside your machine can reach it.
      </p>

      <aside class="aside measure mt-5">
        Safari will not connect to a local runner. It blocks an https page from
        reaching <code>http://localhost</code> and, unlike Chrome and Firefox,
        makes no exception for your own machine. There is no fix worth the
        trouble on your side — use Chrome or Firefox for this, or deploy a
        runner instead, which is https and works everywhere.
      </aside>

      <h2>Deploying one instead</h2>

      <p>
        Worth it if you cannot install Python where you are reading — a work
        machine you do not control, a Chromebook, an iPad — or if you would
        rather not install PyTorch at all. It is also the only option that
        works in Safari.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href={SITE.railway.url} rel="noopener">Railway</a> builds the
        Dockerfile in <code>public/runner/</code> straight from a fork of this
        repository. New Project → Deploy from GitHub repo → set the root
        directory to <code>public/runner</code>, then add one service variable
        before the first deploy:
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`WELLDUN_TOKEN=<a long random string of your own>`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The runner refuses to start on a public address without it. Connect with
        your service's URL and <code>?token=</code> plus what you chose.
      </p>

      {
        SITE.railway.paid && (
          <p class="claim mt-7 !border-graphite !font-sans !text-base !font-normal !tracking-normal">
            That Railway link is a referral link. If you sign up through it you
            get $20 of credit and I earn a commission on what you spend for the
            first year. It costs you nothing extra, and it is the reason to say
            plainly that the free option above this one is faster.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <h2>What you are agreeing to</h2>

      <p>
        The runner executes whatever the page sends it. That is the feature, and
        it is worth reading the file — it is one page of standard library —
        before you run it.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Locally</strong>, it runs as you, on your files. Stop it when
          you are done.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deployed</strong>, it is an endpoint on the public internet
          that runs arbitrary Python, and the token is the only thing in front of
          it. Anyone who gets that token can run anything on your bill — and
          mining or reselling compute is exactly what Railway's fair-use policy
          prohibits, so it would be your account that answers for it. Keep the
          token out of screenshots, and delete the service when you finish.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The page will not connect to anything but your own machine without
        asking you first, by name, and will not accept a runner address over
        plain http. Neither of those is paranoia: a connect URL is a thing
        somebody could hand you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Shell>
